# With --parallel, a request or a decoding stream that needed rows the pool had free, but not in one range, evicted
# kept prompts (least recently used first) until a free range opened, and compacted only after evicting every one.
# Two long conversations taking turns (two agents at once) then evicted each other: a turn whose extent could not
# grow in place dropped the other conversation's only state, and its next turn read its whole history again (150-210K
# tokens, 2-3 minutes). Now the pool compacts as soon as its free rows cover the request (for a growing extent, the
# extents up to it slide down and the ones after it up, so the free rows follow it; each moves once at most), and
# evicts only while they fall short. A turn of one conversation may then move the other's rows: a copy on the GPU,
# against a prefill of the evicted conversation. Same replies: placement only, the moved rows are the same bytes.
# By @ezoushen (PR #62).

